<p><strong>Item #:</strong> SCP-1683</p>
<p><strong>Object Class:</strong> Safe</p>
<p><strong>Special Containment Procedures:</strong> The entrance to SCP-1683 is to be sealed and the interior monitored by security personnel. Only D-Class personnel are permitted into SCP-1683's interior. A containment site has been set up in the residence connected to SCP-1683, with several other homes in the neighborhood having been purchased in order to facilitate containment. A cover story regarding ongoing construction work has been implemented, pending further developments in SCP-1683's containment method.</p>
<p>During activation events, all Foundation personnel are required to wear ear and eye protection, and not to come within 20 meters of SCP-1683. In the event that a member of site personnel suffers a hearing injury, the inability to follow proper procedure shall be noted on their disciplinary record.</p>
<p><strong>Description:</strong> SCP-1683 is a child's bedroom, located on the second floor of a two-story residential home in . SCP-1683 contains one furnished bed, a dresser, one telescope, a ceiling covered in luminescent outer-space-themed stickers, and several posters and books regarding space exploration. Notably, the residence's second floor contains no windows. Analysis has shown that they accurately map many known star systems and planetary locations, although only ones known when SCP-1683 was created.</p>
<p>Subjects who enter SCP-1683 will immediately and permanently have a fascination with astronomy. Testing has shown that one D-Class personnel exposed to SCP-1683 was affected for a full year, prior to termination. Typically, subjects will begin to observe the stickers within SCP-1683, and compare them to the books. As the subject continues to observe, the stickers will begin to accurately mimic the current appearance of the night sky, with satellites and other man-made objects not being visible.</p>
<p>Sighted subjects, including those who are legally blind or blind in one eye, will be affected if they contact the telescope. If the subject makes skin contact with the telescope, the door will shut and entry will become impossible. While the door is sealed, the sound of a human subject counting down from ten to one can be heard. This voice will resemble the subject's voice, although in mute subjects the countdown will be identical to recordings of the Apollo 13 launch. Attempts to record what occurs within SCP-1683 results in all footage becoming identical to the television broadcast of the Apollo 13 launch.</p>
<p>Following this, the walls and door around SCP-1683 will rapidly be heated to 3300 degrees Celsius, although no ignition will occur, while sound identical to the Apollo 13 liftoff can be heard. These sounds will not be heard outside of the house SCP-1683 is located in. Notably, astronomy books made after 1971, and any other genre of book made in any time period are unaffected by SCP-1683. Astronomy books from this period will vanish with the subject. Books on astrology, or fiction using &quot;soft&quot; science will be incinerated.</p>
<p>After twenty minutes, it will become possible to enter SCP-1683. There will be no sign of any subjects previously occupying it. At the same time, orbiting satellites will report a human-shaped anomaly in space appearing approximately 2000 kilometers over the surface of the earth, rapidly moving away. Analysis has shown that the objects resemble subjects who disappeared within SCP-1683.</p>
<p>SCP-1683 was discovered in 1971, following a set of police reports detailing SCP-1683's effect. The subjects originally residing within SCP-1683, family with two parents and one male child, had gone missing directly prior to the first recorded manifestation of SCP-1683's effect. Neighbors described the son as having a deep interest in space exploration, spending most of his time studying it. It is currently believed that this subject initiated SCP-1683's effect, although how he did so is unknown. All witnesses have been issued Class-C to -B amnestics, depending on their relationship with the subjects.</p>
<p>As of 8/19/1976, SCP-1683 has been classified as Safe.</p>
<p><strong>Addendum:</strong> When SCP-1683 was searched, a document appearing to chart a route from earth to the moon was discovered within a book. This document was heavily worn with eraser marks and changes, indicating that it had been significantly altered several times prior to being discovered. In addition, this writing was discovered on the opposite side.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If there's been a giant leap for mankind, why am I still wasting time here? Tomorrow is waiting, and everything is set. Scopes are set up and the stars are there, watching. I'll see them soon</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Addendum:</strong> On 09/18/2008, Foundation deep-space satellites orbiting Saturn received a transmission, believed to be related to SCP-1683.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hello? Can someone hear this? I need you to take them back. You've been sending the others, right? If you know how it works, or if you've changed it or anything like that, I need you to please take them-<br /></p>
<div style="text-align: center;">
<p><em>(2 minutes of transmission are indecipherable)</em></p>
</div>
<br />
-Neptune won't even take them in. They're not dreamers, and they don't belong here. I know some of your people have been doing well, those are fine. I got lonely here by myself. But my parents have no place here. Take them, before they hurt themselves again?</blockquote>
</div>
